## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design practical, measurable diagnostics for cross-region latency without code changes, focusing on network-layer factors and real-world tooling. It emphasizes data collection, hypothesis-driven testing, and concrete mitigations across DNS, CDN, and edge routing.

## Key Concepts
- Anycast and IXP ECMP behavior under load
- DNS latency vs HTTP latency as diagnostic signals
- TLS SNI routing at CDNs and edge caches
- CDN origin fetch queuing and caching strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple latency data parser (conceptual)
const fs = require('fs');
const rows = fs.readFileSync('latency.csv','utf8').trim().split('\n');
rows.slice(1).forEach(r => {
  const [region, dns, http] = r.split(',');
  console.log(region, parseFloat(dns), parseFloat(http));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate this across regions to detect anomalies in near real-time?
- Which dashboards or alerts would you implement to surface ECMP churn vs CDN backlog quickly?

## Concept
HTTP/2 suffers from head-of-line blocking at the TCP level - a single lost packet blocks all streams. QUIC solves this by using UDP as the transport protocol and implementing reliability, congestion control, and stream multiplexing at the application layer.

## Implementation
```go
// QUIC stream independence
for _, stream := range conn.Streams() {
    go func(s quic.Stream) {
        // Each stream processes independently
        data, err := io.ReadAll(s)
        // Lost packets only affect this stream
        handleStreamData(s.ID(), data)
    }(stream)
}

// Connection-level recovery
conn.HandleLostPackets(func(packetID uint64) {
    // Selective retransmission
    if shouldRetransmit(packetID) {
        conn.RetransmitPacket(packetID)
    }
})
```

## Trade-offs
- **Pros**: Stream independence, faster recovery, reduced latency
- **Cons**: Higher CPU usage, UDP firewall issues, complex implementation

